Date: Fri, 25 Jun 1993 11:07:14 -0700 (PDT)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: LING:  Critiquing Chomsky

	Well, this is ragged and full of gaps, but it's already way
too long ...

Simon! asks:

> So, although (or rather *because*) I have strong objections to Chomsky's work, I would 
> appreciate hearing from Scott (or anyone else) who can offer a counter-argument to some 
> specific thing that Chomsky has said about language.

A tall order, given the nature of much of Chomsky's rhetoric.  For example,
let's try a recent specific claim (from _Barriers_, p. 4):

     Consider next the theory of movement.  Assume that there are
     two types of movement:  substitution and adjunction. 
     Substitution has the following general properties ...

p. 5:

     Assume that movement is never determined by a specific rule,
     but rather results from the interaction of other factors in
     the manner described in the references cited above.

Now first off, notice that these claims are "assumed".  But this
is Chomsky's normal mode of argumentation, and since these claims
are, in fact, essential features of Chomsky's (ca. early 80's)
theory, we may as well take them as assertions and see if we want
to argue with them.
     What he's talking about here is the idea that in a sentence
like:

     What did you get?

_what_ has been moved out of its "underlying", "original", or
"logical" position as the object of _get_.  In Transformational
Grammar (which I'm using as a cover term for Chomsky's first and
second theories of syntax, the second being in effect the
original version of Chomskyan generative grammar), there was one
movement rule to handle sentences like this, another to handle
sentences like:

     Klaus!'s posts I really enjoy.

(where "underlyingly" the noun phrase _Klaus!'s posts_ "belongs"
in the object position after _enjoy_), still another to get the
"underlying" object into subject position in passive sentences
like:

     Generative grammar has been accepted by many linguists.

etc.  What Chomsky is proposing in these quotes is that rather than
having in the grammar a list of movement rules, as in the
transformational account, these are all a single process, with the
differences among them predictable from other principles of the
grammar.
     But this proposal is meaningful only in the context in which
a separation between underlying representations and actual
sentences, mapped to one another by rules which include some that
moving a constituent from one position to another, is already a
part of the theory.  If you start with a simple-minded
commonsense position that knowledge of a language involves simply
learning a set of sentence patterns, then the idea of "movement"
in these examples may be a useful metaphor, but doesn't have any
theoretical reality.  If you subscribe to a theory (and there are
several of these around now, steadily gaining ground) in which
each word carries its own specifications for how it combines with
others--so that, for example, words like _what_ and _who_ are
lexically specified to always come at the beginning of their
clause--the idea of "movement" is hard to interpret, even
metaphorically.  More generally, if you don't start with the
assumption of two distinct levels of structure linked by a set of
rules (and there are several such theories around), there is no
sense in arguing about the details of how "movement" takes place.
     So unless you accept a number of important background
assumptions to start with, Chomsky's suggestion is not right or
wrong, but simply meaningless.  So it's entirely possible--and
quite common--for linguists to dismiss _Barriers_ (for example)
without bothering to list specific claims that they disagree
with; the disagreements are at a more fundamental level,
regarding claims which are assumed rather than overtly expressed
in the book.

     So what we need to deal with is the more basic claims. 
Let's go back to the classics.  

>From the Introduction to _Logical Structure of Linguistic Theory_
(p. 5):

     A language L is understood to be a set (in general infinite)
     of finite strings of symbols drawn from a finite "alphabet". 
     Each such string is a sentence of L. ...  A grammar of L is
     a system of rules that specifies the set of sentences of L
     and assigns to each sentence a structural description. ...
     It is appropriate, in my opinion, to regard the grammar of L
     as a representation of fundamental aspects of the knowledge
     of L possessed by the speaker-hearer who has mastered L.

_Syntactic Structures_, p. 13:

     From now on I will consider a _language_ to be a set (finite
     or infinite) of sentences, each finite in length and
     constructed out of a finite set of elements.   ...
     The fundamental aim in the linguistic analysis of a language
     L is to separate _grammatical_ sequences from
     _ungrammatical_ sequences which are not sentences of L and
     to study the structure of the grammatical sequences.  The
     grammar of L will thus be a device that generates all of the
     grammatical sequences of L and none of the ungrammatical
     ones.

_Current Issues in Linguistic Theory_, pp. 9-10:

     The grammar as a whole can thus be regarded as, ultimately,
     a device for pairing phonetically represented signals with
     semantic interpretations, this pairing being mediated
     through a system of abstract structures generated by the
     syntactic component.

     From this formalist view of the goals of linguistics we also
get Chomsky's peculiar notion of "explanation":

     On a much deeper ... level (that of explanatory adequacy) a
     grammar is justified to the extent that it is a _principled_
     descriptively adequate system, in that the linguistic theory
     with which it is associated selects this grammar over others
     ... (_Aspects_ p. 27)

That is, a formal system which generates the correct set of
sentences for a language explains the facts of that language. 
This notion of "explanation" does have some adherents among
philosophers of science (Stephen Toulmin is one, if I remember
right), but it is not satisfactory in any science with empirical
content.  The claim that a generative theory of the sort that
Chomsky is pursuing explains linguistic data is equivalent to
saying that D'Arcy Thompson's "Laws of Form" explain the shapes
of fishes, or that Mendel's ratios explain the inheritance of
characteristics.  Some of the work done in the Chomskyan
framework is very useful in describing and categorizing
linguistic data, but it is simply fraudulent to claim that it is
an explanatory theory--if any of the Chomskyan claims about
universal aspects of language (e.g. notions like subjaceny, or
the generalizations of X' theory) are in fact correct (which has
yet to be demonstrated for *any* Chomskyan claims, the best-
documented of which have been shown to be arguable for a dozen or
so languages, mostly European, out of the several thousand
currently spoken in the world), an explanatory theory of
linguistics will need to provide an account of *why*.  (Why,
e.g., should all syntactic categories include a Spec?)  But this
level of explanation is something that Chomsky and his followers
are aggressively uninterested in.
     IMO this view of language as an abstract formal system, and
of the goal of linguistics as being to characterize this abstract
system, is the fundamental error from which the irrelevance of
most of Chomsky's linguistic thought stems.  Keep in mind that
Chomksy is not advocating simply a mathematical metalinguistics,
but a theory of human language--the claim is not that there might
be something interesting to be found by regarding a language as a
formal system and trying to characterize it mathematically, but
that *what is represented in the mind of someone who knows a
language* *is* (no General Semanticist Chomsky, no indeed) an
abstract formal system.  I.e. that knowledge of a language (on
the part of a human speaker) is equivalent to formal
specification of a formal system.  Again, this is like asserting
that the "Laws of Form", as applied e.g. to the shapes of fish,
are the fundamentals of a theory of evolution.
     And from this follows the basic article of faith which
unites all generative grammarians--the "logical problem of
language acquisition"  (also known as "the projection problem" or
"Plato's problem").  Since children learning language do not have
access to the kind of information that would be necessary to
induce a formal system of this level of complexity, something
else must be happening.  Chomsky's route out of this is to assume
that significant aspects of the system are innately available,
and thus don't need to be derived inductively from linguistic
input in the course of language acquisition.  He appears to
assume (though he avoids saying much about it, as far as I know)
that the general learning mechanism, i.e. whatever it is we have
that lets us learn theoretical physics, litcrit theory,
morphogenetics, or gematria, has little inherent structure, so
that the innate structure that contributes to language
acquisition must belong to a specialized system dedicated to
language:

     We may inquire into the mechanisms by which the system was
     acquired.  If, in fact, acquisition of this system is, like
     acquisition of language, a normal biological function of
     humans, we may seek to characterize the initial state that
     is a common human attribute.  The empirical condition that
     must be met by this characterization is that a "device" in
     this initial state, given data of the sort available to a
     person who acquires a given system of knowledge or belief,
     will attain a final state in which this system is
     represented.  The general theory that characterizes this
     initial state, if it meets the empirical condition, is an
     explanatory theory of a particular human cognitive faculty.
     (_LSLT_ p. 10)


This is where the argument breaks down, and there's no sense in
worrying about any other arguments until this is dealt with.  All
of Chomsky's theories (and over the last 40 years he's had
several) start from the assumption that human beings acquire a
generative system which explicitly characterizes all and only the
grammatical sentences of a language as formal objects, without
reference to their meaning or function.  (One reason why it is
difficult to argue with Chomskyans is that many of them are
unable to conceptualize the issues in any other terms).
     There is only the most marginal evidence that this notion is
even plausible.  The assumption that the idea of a language as a
set of sentences has any psychological relevance is simply a
matter of faith.  Speakers in fact do not and cannot give
consistent and replicable judgements as to which strings are and
are not sentences of their language in this sense.  Anyone who
has ever actually payed attention to a child acquiring language
(and this includes most--though unfortunately not quite all--
specialists in language acquisition) knows that what *appears* to
be happening is that the child is acquiring not some kind of
abstract grammar, but a set of strategies for communicating with
others--it is quite clearly, at least in the initial stages, a
task-driven process.  The simplest explanation for the process by
which children progress from primitive manipulative utterances to
a linguistic ability similar to that of adults in their community
is the interaction of this task-driven process with the same
imitative proclivities by which they learn other behavioral norms
of their community.


