Date: Fri, 25 Jun 1993 20:07:35 -0600 (MDT)
From: Stanton McCandlish <anton@hydra.unm.edu>
Subject: MEDIA: Jurassic Park

Well, I retract my criticism of the criticism that JP is anti-science.  It
does seem to be so, sadly.  I thought you were over reacting, but no. 
Still, it's a real mindblower.   AMAZING graphics.  I read somewhere
(_Wired_ #2 maybe?) that in 1999, people will look back at the LA riots
and wonder how just a video tape could have caused such a mess.  I tend to
believe it.  Pretty Soon Now, we'll be able to simulate on video anything,
with a very high degree of realism, if the progress made with JP is any
kind of indicator.  

Dispite it being an amazing eye-feast, some other probs with the film:

It is never explained how they created extinct plants (remember the leaf,
right before the protagonists saw their 1st dino?).  The dinos were made
from blood in amber-preserved mosquitos...I guess the idea is that they
used preserved plant material and directly cloned it.  Is this believable,
from what we now know about cloning?

It suffers from what I call the "Alien Syndrome": a race or other group of
creatures that are fast, strong and unexpectedly intelligent, manifest
all of their skills in trying to kill every human being in sight, yet
show AMAZING stupidity in every other aspect.  Would a veloceraptor
attack a T.rex?  I don't think so.  Would a veloceraptor go after humans
despite being caged by them, shot at, etc etc, in a totally foreign
environment (the inside of a building?) Huh-uh.  Whoever made this film
shows an amazing ignorance of what motivates animals.  I can see the
dinos going after humans if they were hungry AND reasonably assured of
success.  But considering that all the predators we know tend to get away
from humans as quickly as possible, and are scared shitless of fire and
guns and loud noises, its much much more likely, as far as I am
concerned, that the veloceraptors would have run like hell.  I won't even
bring up the cage attack sequence at the start, that was just silly.  And
besides, the dinos didn't seem the least interested in EATING people just
hunting them down and killing them.  Sorry, but that's a HUMAN trait...

Another thing is the wires.  If the dino had been used to a REAL nasty
shock from the wires, the chances of them just happening to test them,
find them not electrified, and escape, RIGHT when the power goes down, are
pretty darned slim.  And the veloceraptor escape was also silly.  How did
they get up those walls and tear thru the wires like that?  Why would they
try, unless they are psychic and know the wires are not electrified?

Another really dumb thing is the lack of security.  Cars that you can't
drive, no backup computer system, etc.  Ever been to a themepark?  Every
contingency is covered, else the park gets sued by ever schmoe who comes
along. 

The hunter, fat-guy, and ludicrous chaos-mathematician characters were
totally worthless, and added nothing to the plot.  Neither did the lawyer,
except that his death scene was a laugh riot.  Goldblum's character was
not even original but ported, mannerisms and all, right from _The_Fly_ and
_Buckaroo_Banzai_. 

The plot was almost 100% predictable (only one of my predictions for who'd
live and who'd get munched was wrong, since the old guy got away.)

Why frog DNA?  At least lizard DNA would've matched a little better.

Would a T.rex really make THAT much noise and shaking-around as it walked?
Could it really move that fast?  I think not, on both counts.  A review of
Gene Wolfe's essay on giant-size creatures tends to support my view (that
essay is in _Castle_of_the_Otter, which as far as I know is only available
from the Science Fiction Book Club; it's a companion to his "New Sun"
series - _Shadow_of_the_Torturor_, etc.)

What was the point of the stolen embryo can falling into the river?  What
the hell was that guy doing in the park anyway? Wasn't he supposed to be
meeting some contact at the boat?

And finally, the most glaring Dumb Thing of all: there was no reason in
hell to clone carnivorous dinosaurs.  It was totally foolhardy, and any
themepark would be quite cognizant of that fact.  The whole thing could've
been avoided if they'd just made herbivorous dinos.  Duhh.

Besides that, one question: did you notice when the herd of ostrich-like
dinos was attacked by the T.rex, the main character admonishes the kiddos
to watch.  The little boy got some funky look on his face as if he'd
attained enlightenment, and said something like "I see it now" or
something, and then said "so much blood..."  What the hell was that
supposed to be?  I get the feeling something's been cut there.

And the electrocution scene: would the kid have been knocked off the fence
like that, or just fried?  I don't know enough about high-voltage wires to
know the answer.

Despite this stuff, the film's great!  Best dino movie ever, and the
graphics are stunning.  Scared the hell out of me too!

Anyone heard any news on the REAL effort to get dino DNA from amberified
mosquitos?  I just saw one brief mention of it in a mag, but nothing since.
