Date: Fri, 25 Jun 93 19:41:44 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Drexler's personality...

Phil Fraering writes:

> "Young Hiro is *so* bright but he needs to work harder on 
> his cooperation skills."

I presume this is from "Snow Crash"?

Let me say that I wasn't trying to bash Eric Drexler's personality,
just note some interesting things about nanotech, the personality cult
that has grown up around him in the last ten years, and why, perhaps,
nanotech research is not progressing very rapidly.

Like several of you, I've known Eric for some years...in my case,
since around 1986-7. I'm not in his inner circle (which I define to be
those invited to serve on the Boards of Directors of Foresight and the
Institute for Molecular Manfufacturing, those in his "kitchen
cabinet," and those who faithfully go to all the nanotech happenings,
often acting as gophers).

Eric is a fine person, and very bright, too. (Surprise!) But, in my
opinion (never was this usually redundant qualifier more important),
he has become somewhat "messianic" about the implications of nanotech.
Hardly surprising, though. After all, many of _us_ are semi-messianic
about our interests, and this depite not having invented an entire
field of study!

And Eric is immersed  nearly full-time in this stuff, has his
followers all feeding him with praise about his achievements (which is
also not surprising, and is even justifiable....but it also has some
drawbacks, well familiar to the Greeks and Romans), and has a vision
of a nanotech future which, in my opinion (again), pretty much dooms
him to being frustrated at the actual rate of progress.

That's all I'll say. Some have said I should go easy on Drexler--not
that I think I'm being very tough, especially since he has long
advocated "decision duels" and "fact forums," though they never quite
seem to happen in nanotech!--and that I should "not reveal the secrets
of the temple" (my words, but this was the sense I got out of the
comments).

Well, Eric Drexler is an important figure of our time, and especially
of our group, and is not an "unmentionable."

If my comments ever sound personally vindictive, spiteful, or envious,
I hope readers will tell me about it. But the general issues of how
great ideas often produce cults, messianic figures, and even paranoia
(a claim I'm not making about E.D.), are key issues and they are fair
game for discussion, just as they would be if we were all meeting in
person.

I wish him well, and I always have. I just have a hard time memorizing
the party line.

-Tim




