> (So I can, in theory, convert any particular random string into a nice
> simple "All 1s" string by XORing the random string with the suitable
> complement string...my "special Turing machine.")

Maybe the key is, here, that practically all finite strings are much
larger than any reasonable Turing machine.  We should only restrict
ourselves to Turing machines that are about the size needed to be
universal.  Then, giving a string that is, say, 10^100 times the size
of some UTM, the only way to come up with a TM that gave it a small
complexity would be to that the TM itself have this huge amount of
information in it, 10^100 times more information than it really
needed.

I for+0get whether Chaitin explicitly restricted himself to TM's
like this.  I know he said that the Algorithmic Information Content of
a number is defined only to within an additive factor of some size.  I
don't recall whether that size is bounded in some way.

