Date: Fri, 25 Jun 93 22:24:46 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: "Algorithmic Information and Evolution"

About a year ago we had an active list member named Kenneth "Tom" Tolman.
He was really into this AIT stuff and in fact had published on the topic.

Tom argued that the proper measure for the complexity of a physical
object (kind of like Bennett's "logical depth") was the number of non-
deterministic events that had gone into its history.

This was a rather non-intuitive definition, but his reasoning was that
deterministic processes cannot create new information in the AIT sense.
He gave the example of Conway's "Life" game, the best known cellular
automata simulation.

Conway's Life uses deterministic rules.  That means that, given a starting
pattern with a certain information content using AIT, any resulting pattern
has basically the same AIT information.  That's because you can get the
final pattern from the initial pattern + a Life program, and Life programs
aren't very big.

In the same way, any program or physical system which operates using
only deterministic rules cannot really create new information.  In
particular, if the universe were deterministic (which it appears not to
be, of course), then no new information could be created.

An interesting question is whether life (ordinary life, now) could evolve
in a deterministic universe.  I don't see why it couldn't.  In fact, I
would think life could evolve in Life.  But this raises the question of
how we could reconcile the apparent increase in information content caused
by evolution with the impossibility of increasing information content
in a deterministic system.

Tolman, unfortunately, had taken his ideas a little too far, IMO, and in
fact had pretty well gone off the deep end with them.  He was convinced that
the purpose of life was to increase complexity, and furthermore that he
had proved that there existed absolute standards of Good and Bad, where
Good was anything which increased complexity (by his definition).  Thus,
destruction of the rain forests is Bad, etc.

Still, I think the deterministic/non-deterministic distinction is useful.
Tim, in his genetic algorithm example, started with a simple string with
low information content, and ended with a complex one.  But some non-
deterministic processes seem to have occured in between.  Tolman would
(I think) point to those as the source of the increased complexity, not the
GA process itself.

Hal

